--- !u!114 &436308603
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 436308601}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 308e3640226ceb346b1cfcac31db9c62, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: '::'
  steps:
  - targets:
    - {fileID: 729869442}
    infoText: Every station has an Emergency Stop within reach of the operator. Near
      the Controller-USB there's an E-Stop that cuts power to the arm immediately
      if something goes wrong while you're at the controls.
    instructionsText: Point at and select the Emergency Stop control near the Controller-USB
      to continue.
    RequiredOccupiedTable: {fileID: 0}
    requiredActionId: 
    passiveAction: 0
    hintTarget: {fileID: 712449761}
  - targets:
    - {fileID: 985069602}
    infoText: "The ASRS Storage Unit itself also has its own Emergency Stop, mounted
      on the rack housing \u2014 reachable even if you're standing at the rack instead
      of the controller. Real installations wire every E-Stop together so pressing
      any one of them halts the whole cell."
    instructionsText: Point at and select the Emergency Stop button on the ASRS Storage
      Unit to continue.
    RequiredOccupiedTable: {fileID: 0}
    requiredActionId: 
    passiveAction: 0
    hintTarget: {fileID: 444825747}
  - targets:
    - {fileID: 720042980}
    infoText: "This is the pneumatic gripper \u2014 the \"hand\" on the end of the
      robotic arm. It's what actually grabs a template by its handle and carries
      it between the rack and the conveyor."
    instructionsText: Point at and select the pneumatic gripper on the robot arm
      to continue.
    RequiredOccupiedTable: {fileID: 0}
    requiredActionId: 
    passiveAction: 0
    hintTarget: {fileID: 1379100314}
  - targets:
    - {fileID: 1910548497}
    infoText: "This is the RFID sensor. As a slotted table passes it, the sensor
      reads the table's tag and reports its identity \u2014 that's how the ASRS always
      knows which template is which without a person having to look."
    instructionsText: Point at and select the RFID sensor to continue.
    RequiredOccupiedTable: {fileID: 0}
    requiredActionId: 
    passiveAction: 0
    hintTarget: {fileID: 749321901}
  - targets: []
    infoText: "The SCORBASE software runs on the station laptop \u2014 it's the operator's
      window into the Controller-USB. Nothing else in this lesson works until it's
      powered on."
    instructionsText: Turn on the laptop to continue.
    RequiredOccupiedTable: {fileID: 0}
    requiredActionId: laptop_power_on
    passiveAction: 0
    hintTarget: {fileID: 0}
  - targets: []
    infoText: "This is the ASRS Controller-USB. Per the manual's Powering Up Controller-USB
      procedure (p.13): turn on the controller, reboot the station PC, launch SCORBASE,
      and confirm it comes up in On-Line mode. It runs the SCORBASE software and
      is what actually drives the ASRS robotic arm \u2014 SCORBASE talks to it over
      USB, and it talks to the arm's motors directly."
    instructionsText: Press the power switch on the Controller-USB to turn it on,
      then confirm SCORBASE comes online.
    RequiredOccupiedTable: {fileID: 0}
    requiredActionId: controller_power_on
    passiveAction: 0
    hintTarget: {fileID: 753191832}
  - targets: []
    infoText: "SCORBASE won't talk to the robot until it's actually connected. Click
      Go Online to establish communication between the software and the Controller-USB
      \u2014 confirm it reads Online before attempting to move or home the robot."
    instructionsText: On the SCORBASE panel, press Go Online and confirm the status
      reads ONLINE.
    RequiredOccupiedTable: {fileID: 0}
    requiredActionId: scorbase_online
    passiveAction: 0
    hintTarget: {fileID: 0}
  - targets: []
    infoText: "Per the manual's Homing the Robot from the SCORBASE Software procedure
      (p.15) \u2014 before SCORBASE can trust any position, it needs to know exactly
      where every axis actually is. Search Home drives each axis to a known reference
      point, one at a time, so its position becomes trustworthy. Once homed, the
      robot sits at its saved Home Position \u2014 #110, coordinates 0, 0, 0."
    instructionsText: On the SCORBASE panel, press Search Home and watch each axis
      check off, then Robot.
    RequiredOccupiedTable: {fileID: 0}
    requiredActionId: scorbase_home
    passiveAction: 0
    hintTarget: {fileID: 0}
  - targets: []
    infoText: "Real SCORBASE keeps Manual Movement under View > Manual Movement \u2014
      the jog controls below are that same panel. Use Manual Movement whenever you
      need to position or test the robot by hand rather than commanding it straight
      to a saved position."
    instructionsText: On the SCORBASE panel, use any of the Manual Movement jog buttons
      (Z, Y, or X) to move the arm.
    RequiredOccupiedTable: {fileID: 0}
    requiredActionId: manual_movement_used
    passiveAction: 0
    hintTarget: {fileID: 0}
  - targets: []
    infoText: "Per the manual's Testing the ASRS procedure (p.16) \u2014 with the
      arm homed, you can now command it to pick up any stored table by its Table
      ID through the Pick and Place section, the same idea as SCORBASE's GP (Go to
      Position) command driving the robot to a saved position number (e.g. GP #499
      \u2192 480.17, 486.16, 14.13). This is the same kind of check a real installation
      runs before trusting the ASRS with real parts."
    instructionsText: On the SCORBASE panel's Pick and Place section, type a stored
      table's ID (e.g. 070001) into Source ID and press OK.
    RequiredOccupiedTable: {fileID: 0}
    requiredActionId: test_move
    passiveAction: 0
    hintTarget: {fileID: 0}
  - targets: []
    infoText: "A full test also proves the arm can reach the far side of the rack,
      which requires it to rotate before moving \u2014 exactly like the real ASRS-36x2
      switching sides. "
    instructionsText: Rotate the Side of the arm and now enter a stored table's ID
      on the other side of the rack (row 1 through 6, e.g. 010001) as the Source
      ID and press OK again.
    RequiredOccupiedTable: {fileID: 0}
    requiredActionId: test_move
    passiveAction: 0
    hintTarget: {fileID: 0}
  - targets: []
    infoText: "This is the real material flow, run as a Standalone Pick-and-Place
      Operation: a slotted table rides the conveyor into the ASRS station, the RFID
      sensor identifies it, and the arm picks it and stores it in an empty cell \u2014
      using the RFID Reader as its destination. Every move the arm makes here still
      crosses back through the center position before heading to the next one, and
      it won't place a table into the scanner while another is already sitting there.
      Watching it happen end to end is the real test of everything you've just set
      up."
    instructionsText: Load a slotted table/template onto the conveyor belt, and wait
      for the arm to take it from the conveyor and store it in the rack.
    RequiredOccupiedTable: {fileID: 0}
    requiredActionId: conveyor_returned
    passiveAction: 1
    hintTarget: {fileID: 0}
  infoPanel: {fileID: 493804593}
  nextButton: {fileID: 1223861148}
  hintShowcase: {fileID: 712449764}
  hintButton: {fileID: 0}
  completionText: "Nice work \u2014 you've identified the ASRS's safety and handling
    components, brought SCORBASE online, proven the arm can reach the whole rack,
    and watched a template make it all the way from the conveyor into storage."
  quizManager: {fileID: 1431878561}
  onSequenceComplete:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
